WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.840
(dramatic music)

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:04.120
- [Narrator] February 12, 1988,

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.040
the dining staff aboard
this cruise liner

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.000
are making final preparations

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.160
ahead of tonight's
gala dinner service.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.000
For one couple,
tonight's dinner

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.200
also marks the end of
a very special occasion

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.280
as 26-year-old Ashley and
new husband Charlie celebrate

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.000
the last night of their
honeymoon cruise to Costa Rica.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.320
But unfortunately
for the new couple,

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:36.360
their happily ever after is
about to be cut tragically short

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.080
because later that
evening Ashley disappears.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.680
(dramatic music)

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.280
What happened to Ashley?

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:52.840
Is it a terrible accident,
or is it a conspiracy

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.800
by secret government
agents to commit murder?

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.280
How did she end up dead?

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.360
(dramatic music)

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.600
(monitor beeping)

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.880
(suspenseful music)

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.080
(dramatic music)
(birds chirping)

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.280
It's day one of the latest
voyage of this cruise ship.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.120
(camera flashing)

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.200
At an incredible 635-feet long,

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.200
with many first-class amenities,

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.800
this ship is one of the most

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.800
beautiful vessels
in the world,

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:20.800
taking guests from California

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.200
down the Pacific Coast
to Costa Rica.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.880
But unfortunately
for new bride, Ashley,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.200
this week long cruise
through paradise

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.880
will be the last
trip of her life.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.800
(dramatic music)

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.280
Ashley is celebrating
her honeymoon

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.240
with new husband, Charlie,

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:50.200
along with over 2500 guests,
crew members, and staff.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.120
(people chatting)

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.320
But could one of them
be responsible

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:56.200
for her disappearance?

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.120
(camera flashing)

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.960
(people chatting)

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.120
(dramatic music)

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.360
Ashley grew up in Louisiana,

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.840
and is described by her
friends as a kindhearted

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.240
and soft spoken young woman.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.800
But despite her
mild-mannered attitude,

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.280
years of modern dance, ballet,

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.640
and intense physical
fitness training

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.080
have sculpted her petite
five-foot four-inch frame

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.000
into that of a strong,
flexible athlete.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.240
- I think since she was a
little girl she loved fitness.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.880
She started out
actually with ballet,

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.400
and she went into
fitness classes

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.120
simply because she
was a very shy girl,

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.000
and she wanted to
bolster her confidence.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.280
- [Narrator] After graduating
from massage therapy training

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.560
in Baton Rouge, Louisiana,

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.680
Ashley starts a career
as a massage therapist,

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.560
taking a position at a
Louisiana Country Club.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.920
And it's here in June of 1988

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.880
that Ashley meets Charlie.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.800
Charlie was born in Boston.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.280
He meets Ashley
as a client first,

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:21.920
going in for some treatment
after falling down some stairs.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.960
Ashley is immediately attracted

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.200
to her handsome muscular client,

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.760
who works as a
chiropractor in New Mexico.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.200
- He's on her turf,

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:40.800
but they're both single,

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:41.760
they're both young,

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.600
they're both attractive.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.280
It's the perfect opportunity
for something to happen.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.120
- [Narrator] Ashley and
Charlie quickly realize

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.120
they have a lot in common,
including both of them

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:55.240
being passionate about
physical fitness.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.520
For Charlie, his
fall down the stairs

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.600
means that falling in love
has a whole new meaning.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.160
On February 4th,
Ashley and Charlie

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:10.040
drive his red
convertible to Las Vegas

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:12.680
and marry in a small
civil ceremony.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:15.680
(people applauding)

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.160
- Well, Ashley was
still very young

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.200
when she decided
to marry Charlie,

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:24.760
and we were wanting
her to take more time.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:27.760
But after a nine-month
whirlwind romance,

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.320
her heart was set,
so what could we say?

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:33.040
(gentle dramatic music)

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.000
- [Narrator] Two days later,
the Gerbers board a cruise ship

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.160
and start their seven-day
honeymoon cruise to Costa Rica.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.680
(gentle dramatic music)

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:45.200
(birds chirping)

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.800
They're the perfect
all-American couple.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:49.240
Beautiful and successful,

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:52.920
they even take the time to
exercise throughout the week.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:57.800
Guests sit in
their lounge chairs

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.240
and watch them go through
their exercise routine.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.200
(people chatting)

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:09.200
This couple seems destined
for a happy life together.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.200
(dramatic music)

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:15.320
But at dinner on
February 12th,

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:17.800
guests notice some tension

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:21.640
between the picture-perfect
couple.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:23.080
- On the last night
of the honeymoon

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.120
Charlie still wants
everything to be perfect.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:26.640
He still wants them
to keep to their

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.240
perfect eating and
fitness routine.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.280
Whereas, his wife
is a little more,

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:32.760
"Hey, let's live a little.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.200
"It the last night
of our honeymoon."

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:37.000
(people chatting)

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.880
- [Narrator] Charlie
also isn't happy

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:41.720
about Ashley's
appetite for dessert.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:46.800
- And that's when there
starts to be problems.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:49.840
- [Narrator] Charlie's
outbursts gets the attention

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:53.720
of the other guests, including
some of the dining staff,

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:55.920
causing Ashley to run
out of the dining room

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:57.160
with tears in her eyes.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:01.280
(dramatic music)

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.280
(suspenseful music)

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.880
As Charlie chases after Ashley,

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:19.880
he gets into another argument

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:21.960
with a woman on the
deck of the boat.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:28.280
When Charlie catches
up with Ashley

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:29.880
in their honeymoon cabin,

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:32.160
the lover's quarrel
doesn't last long.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:36.040
- When couples
first get together,

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:38.600
there's that sort
of honeymoon phase,

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:40.240
and that may dissolve quickly

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:42.840
when small differences
in their personalities

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:44.280
start to show through.

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:51.160
- So it's the last night
of their honeymoon cruise,

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:53.560
and they decide, "Let's
blow off some tension.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:55.720
"Let's reconnect to
the thing that bonds us

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:58.720
"and go for late-night
run on the track."

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:02.560
(dramatic music)

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:08.560
- [Narrator] Eventually
Charlie's stops

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:10.800
to catch his breath,

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:14.120
but Ashley decides she wants
to get in a few more laps.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:22.800
(suspenseful music)

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:33.280
(dramatic music)

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.280
But unfortunately for Ashley,

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:40.000
this last lap will be the last
time anyone sees her alive.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:47.680
(suspenseful music)

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.000
(dramatic music)

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:11.160
Later that night,
Charlie bursts into

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:14.240
the Captain's quarters
with horrific news.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:16.600
His wife has gone overboard.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:18.720
(muffled voice speaking
on radio)

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:22.040
(dramatic music)

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:26.120
Ashley and Charlie seem to be

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:29.200
the perfect all-American couple.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:33.480
After a whirlwind
romance and marriage,

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:37.240
the pair have set sail
on a cruise ship to enjoy

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:40.880
a week long honeymoon voyage
down the Pacific Coast.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.520
- My husband and I felt that

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.520
she would do things
spontaneously,

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:47.480
and even getting married
was one of those things.

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:49.200
(dramatic music)

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:51.400
- [Narrator] But after an
argument in the dining hall,

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:53.360
Charlie can't help but notice

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:55.120
the other guests watching them,

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:58.480
especially his
beautiful wife, Ashley.

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:06.560
(dramatic music)

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:08.480
But after a late-night jog

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:12.560
on the last night of their
cruise, Ashley disappears.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.560
Is it possible there's
a killer on board?

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:22.560
At 3:00 a.m. on
February the 13th,

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:24.880
the cruise ship's
Captain is interrupted

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:28.560
by a frantic Charlie, who
says that his wife Ashley

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:30.560
has gone overboard.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:35.080
(muffled voice speaking
on radio)

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:37.280
The ship's crew use flashlights

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:39.560
to search for
Ashley in the water.

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:42.560
(suspenseful music)

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:49.320
Charlie is still wearing his
running gear and out of breath.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:53.560
But the Captain also notices

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:55.960
Charlie has injuries
on his face.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:00.000
- We're investigating
a potential homicide.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.120
So when one of the
parties involved

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:06.360
shows up with wounds to
their face scratches, cuts,

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:09.280
it can indicate that there
was some sort of struggle,

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:10.920
some sort of
physical altercation

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:12.440
that resulted in those injuries.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:15.440
- [Narrator] But
before the Captain

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:18.400
can ask how Ashley
went overboard,

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:20.440
Charlie asks to
use the restroom.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:25.440
(dramatic music)

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:27.560
When Charlie goes
into the restroom,

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:31.560
the Captain can see him checking
out and washing his face.

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:34.640
- It's certainly unusual
that you would want

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:39.480
to clean a scratch on your
face before anything else.

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:45.640
- [Narrator] Now with
his face cleaned up,

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:49.480
Charlie is ready to explain
how Ashley went overboard.

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:51.160
(dramatic music)

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:53.480
Charlie tells the Captain
that the two of them

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:56.160
had gone for a late-night
run around the ship.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:03.200
But then tragically,

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:04.840
Charlie says that the wind

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:07.560
starts to pick up
and get stronger.

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:14.440
So strong that it pushes Ashley
over the edge of the ship.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:18.080
She tries her best to hang on,

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:20.520
but the ocean winds
are too much...

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:23.520
(dramatic music)

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:26.360
Sending her plummeting
into the ocean below.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:35.000
Next, the Captain asks about
the cuts on Charlie's face.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:40.640
Charlie says that when he
heard Ashley's screams,

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:42.040
he tried to save her,

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:48.320
but the winds were too strong

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:50.120
and sent him flying into a wall

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:54.360
causing him to hit his
face on a control box.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:58.040
- Well, when you're
on a cruise ship,

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:00.560
you're still at the
mercy of elements,

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:05.560
flash storms, high
winds, violent waves.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:09.480
If the winds got high
enough and strong enough,

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.120
someone might've
been blown overboard.

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:14.040
(dramatic music)

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:15.880
(water splashing)

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:19.120
- [Narrator] Unfortunately,
despite an exhaustive search,

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:22.080
the crew can't locate
Ashley's body in the water,

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:27.400
which means unless
there's any witnesses,

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:34.240
the last person to see
Ashley alive was Charlie.

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:38.040
(gentle music)

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:40.600
But while there's no
evidence of any foul play,

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:42.520
the Captain can't help but feel

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:45.320
like Charlie's story
isn't adding up.

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:49.440
- There's holes in
Charlie's story though.

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:51.520
The Captain looks at
the weather that night,

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:54.000
and the wind speeds for
only five miles an hour.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:57.840
That's nowhere near enough
to blow someone overboard.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:13:59.440
Seas were fairly calm.

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:01.520
They weren't that
rough that night.

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:04.240
So already there's
some suspicion

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:06.080
building about this guy's story.

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:09.520
- [Narrator] All of this
makes the Captain wonder

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:11.920
if there's more to
Charlie's story.

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:14.600
- In this particular case,

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:16.520
the winds were
only reported to be

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:18.480
in the range of
five miles an hour,

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:22.600
which is barely enough
to ruffle your hair.

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:27.200
- [Narrator] Is it possible
that&nbsp;someone else

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:31.120
could have murdered, and
Charlie covered it up?

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:33.880
(dramatic music)

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:47.520
After the Captain
calls to notify police

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:51.160
and the Coast Guard of the
ship's overboard situation,

00:14:51.160 --> 00:14:54.960
he calls in the ship's
medical officer Dr. Price

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.320
to examine Charlie's injuries,

00:14:57.320 --> 00:14:59.200
which proved to be superficial.

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:03.440
(dramatic music)

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:07.200
But then, the Captain
informs Charlie,

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:11.840
that the doctor is also
deputized as a law officer

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:14.520
and would like to interview
Charlie officially

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:17.040
to take down the details
of what happened.

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:22.000
- If the ship is at sea,

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:24.280
the Captain would need
to deputize somebody

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:27.480
or would have a security
officer on board

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:29.520
who would be
responsible for looking

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:31.520
after serious
incidents like this.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:35.360
- [Narrator] Charlie is
willing to be cooperative

00:15:35.360 --> 00:15:38.880
and agrees to let the doctor
record their conversations

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:41.880
and answer his questions
as best as he can.

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:44.480
- Timelines are
incredibly important

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:47.560
when we're talking
about any serious crime,

00:15:47.560 --> 00:15:49.320
particularly homicide.

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:51.280
(people chatting)

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:55.200
Everything leading up to that
event needs to be documented.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:57.120
It needs to be questioned.

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:58.240
Who was involved?

00:15:58.240 --> 00:15:59.560
Who was around?

00:15:59.560 --> 00:16:01.840
Where did things take place,

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:03.560
and what happened
immediately after it?

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:08.560
(tense music)

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:10.400
- [Narrator] While
Charlie sits across

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:12.440
from the doctor to
give his statement,

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:16.200
the Coast Guard begins their
search for Ashley's body.

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:20.400
The question the doctor
wants to answer is

00:16:20.400 --> 00:16:23.200
if it's possible
that Ashley's death

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:25.000
was more than just
a freak accident?

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:29.520
His investigation will
take him all over the ship,

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:34.440
forcing him to ask a
terrifying question.

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:38.200
Is it possible that the
ship has a murderer onboard?

00:16:40.360 --> 00:16:44.240
(suspenseful music)
(water sloshing)

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:50.080
All eyes around the fitness
inspired Ashley and Charlie,

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:52.560
(camera flashing)

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:54.640
who are celebrating
their honeymoon

00:16:54.640 --> 00:16:58.000
with a seven-day voyage
down the Pacific Coast

00:16:58.000 --> 00:16:59.240
on a beautiful cruise ship.

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:05.080
But as the cruise winds down,
some people have noticed

00:17:05.080 --> 00:17:07.800
the once happy couple
aren't so happy.

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:10.160
(dramatic music)

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:12.240
But after a late-night
running session

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:14.600
ends with Ashley
going overboard,

00:17:14.600 --> 00:17:17.560
husband Charlie is
forced to sit down

00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:19.200
with the ship's medical doctor

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:22.880
and a deputized law officer
to explain what happened.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:29.840
(dramatic music)

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:32.920
Charlie says he's willing
to be fully cooperative

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:35.680
and answer any questions
the doctor might have.

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:41.040
He also gives Dr. Price
permission

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:43.000
to keep the cabin door open

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:45.120
and to record audio
of their discussion.

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:50.040
Charlie tells his version

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:52.920
of what happened
earlier that morning.

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:55.040
He sticks to his original story

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:57.520
of how Ashley was out
for a late-night jog,

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:02.920
only to be blown
overboard by strong winds.

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:06.120
(dramatic music)

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:08.080
But as the Captain
noted earlier,

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:10.040
the wind speeds at the time

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:12.920
were only around
five miles per hour.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:14.960
That wouldn't be
enough to blow any

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:17.680
full grown adult
over the railings,

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:20.240
which are three-foot,
six-inches high,

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:23.880
especially someone with
Ashley's athletic background.

00:18:27.040 --> 00:18:30.200
The doctor isn't
buying Charlie's story.

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:32.760
But with no evidence
of his involvement

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:34.280
in a potential murder,

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:37.560
he places Charlie in
an unoccupied cabin

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:41.160
under protective guard while
he continues his investigation.

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:45.200
(gentle dramatic music)

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:49.120
Meanwhile, the Coast Guard
continues searching

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:51.760
the open waters
for Ashley's body.

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:02.240
Then, as the ship continues
to its destination,

00:19:02.240 --> 00:19:05.040
the doctor and the
Captain get a lead

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:06.720
from one of their passengers.

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:13.160
The man turns out to be
an off-duty police officer

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:17.200
and has found what appears to
be one of Ashley's earrings

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:22.120
embedded in the running track
near where she went overboard.

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:29.840
What's more, he's also
found a few strands

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:32.640
of Ashley's hair stuck
to the running track.

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:34.680
(suspenseful music)

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.880
- Protections need
to be put in place

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:39.960
to preserve whatever
evidence may be there

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:41.960
to show whether
it's a crime or not.

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:46.040
So everything needs to
be treated as a homicide

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:48.560
right from the beginning
until you learn it's not.

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:52.120
- [Narrator] With
this new evidence

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:54.720
showing signs of a
possible struggle,

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:58.840
the doctor decides to try
and verify Charlie's claim

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:01.240
that the scratches on his
face were from hitting

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:04.640
a control box during his
attempt to save Ashley.

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:08.600
But upon further inspection,

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:12.200
the doctor can't find any
of Charlie's blood, hair,

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:14.880
or other marks on
the control box.

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:19.040
In fact, it appears to
have no sharp edges at all,

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:23.240
which makes the doctor wonder
if Charlie could be lying

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:25.160
about the source
of his injuries.

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:29.080
- It's one of the
biggest challenges

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:30.360
faced by the
investigators in this case

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:32.160
was there was no
other witnesses.

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:33.840
It was three o'clock
in the morning.

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:35.240
Everyone else was asleep.

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:36.720
So they only have Charlie's word

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:38.960
about what happened
to Ashley.

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:40.800
No one else saw
what was happening.

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:43.560
They were all fast
asleep in their cabins.

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:46.080
(dramatic music)

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:47.520
- [Narrator] The
doctor decides instead

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:49.880
to question some of
the guests to see

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:53.600
if they saw anything suspicious
between Ashley and Charlie

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:57.880
during their trip and before
Ashley's disappearance.

00:20:57.880 --> 00:21:00.160
- But there was something
people witnessed.

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:02.240
There was conflict
on that last night,

00:21:02.240 --> 00:21:04.840
and everyone around
them witnessed

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:07.240
a fight at the dinner
table that night.

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:08.560
(people chatting)

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:10.040
So we do have that.

00:21:10.040 --> 00:21:12.200
We do have that there
was some friction

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:13.960
between them the night she died.

00:21:15.320 --> 00:21:17.200
And there are
witnesses who saw that

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:18.560
and could talk about it.

00:21:21.160 --> 00:21:23.640
And there was also another
argument, an altercation,

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:25.720
that Charlie had with
a female passenger

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:30.960
that evening as well, so...

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:32.840
That could show where
his mood was that night.

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:35.280
Maybe he was already really
angry about something

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:37.240
or just a bit off that day.

00:21:37.240 --> 00:21:39.440
(dramatic music)

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:41.000
- [Narrator] With his
less than stellar alibi,

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:43.240
combined with the
witness testimony,

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:44.920
the doctor is starting to wonder

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:48.440
if Charlie himself
could be responsible

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:49.920
for his wife's disappearance.

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:55.360
But why would what looked
like an innocent argument

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:58.920
send a husband to potentially
murder his new wife

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:00.760
on their honeymoon?

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:04.560
(suspenseful music)

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:05.360
(dramatic music)

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:08.120
But then Dr. Price
gets a surprise visit

00:22:08.120 --> 00:22:10.880
from another passenger, Rachel,

00:22:10.880 --> 00:22:12.800
who claims to have
witnessed Ashley

00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:16.920
being followed by a man the
night of her disappearance.

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:20.960
- But as it turns out, Charlie's
not the only strange man

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:22.440
on board that day.

00:22:25.080 --> 00:22:26.080
There's another fellow.

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:28.000
A woman named
Rachel comes forward

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:30.960
and tells the police that
she was approached by a man

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:34.520
that gave her the creeps
the first day on the cruise.

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:36.640
- [Narrator] This strange
man pressures Rachel

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:40.240
into accepting a drink
from him, but she refuses.

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:43.000
- When she managed to
blow him off and get away,

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:45.680
who does he hit on, but Ashley.

00:22:45.680 --> 00:22:47.480
This guy's really memorable too.

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:50.440
He's so sunburned that
Rachel describes him

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:52.920
as looking like a
bright red tomato.

00:22:52.920 --> 00:22:55.640
(dramatic music)
(people chatting)

00:22:55.640 --> 00:22:58.920
So one thing Rachel observes
is that the sunburned guy

00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:01.520
is almost stalking Ashley

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:06.560
during her time on the
cruise ship, which is weird.

00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:10.000
Makes Rachel really glad she
didn't accept that drink.

00:23:13.040 --> 00:23:15.440
- [Narrator] Unfortunately,
Rachel doesn't know

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:16.920
the man's last name,

00:23:16.920 --> 00:23:18.640
but says the doctor will be able

00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:21.560
to recognize him
because of his sunburn.

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:27.520
The doctor thanks Rachel
for the new information,

00:23:27.520 --> 00:23:31.400
and adds Eric to his list
of possible suspects.

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:36.440
This makes two people who
would have seen Ashley

00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:39.080
after she left the dining hall.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:41.840
(dramatic music)

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:44.680
But with still no witnesses

00:23:44.680 --> 00:23:46.840
for the moment she
went overboard,

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:50.920
Dr. Price can't help but
wonder what if someone else

00:23:50.920 --> 00:23:54.080
is responsible for
Ashley's disappearance.

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:57.880
(people chatting)

00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:00.880
Suddenly, after
hours of searching,

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:04.560
the Coast Guard has tragically
found Ashley's body.

00:24:06.440 --> 00:24:07.920
(water sloshing)

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:10.400
What's more, they've confirmed

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:13.080
this case is a cold
blooded murder,

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:15.960
which means the killer
is still on board.

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:18.960
(suspenseful music)

00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:26.240
Ashley and Charlie are
celebrating their honeymoon

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:29.280
with a seven-day voyage on
a beautiful cruise ship,

00:24:30.640 --> 00:24:33.240
but some of their
daily exercise routines

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:36.080
have caught the eyes of
some other passengers.

00:24:38.960 --> 00:24:41.120
But during a late-night
running session,

00:24:41.120 --> 00:24:44.000
Ashley is mysteriously
thrown overboard

00:24:44.920 --> 00:24:47.040
resulting in one of her earrings

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:51.080
and strands of hair being
found on the ship's deck.

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:53.880
(dramatic music)

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:57.520
Ashley's husband Charlie
tells the ship's doctor,

00:24:57.520 --> 00:24:59.560
the de facto investigator,

00:24:59.560 --> 00:25:01.640
that his wife lost her balance

00:25:01.640 --> 00:25:04.160
after heavy winds blew
her over the edge.

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:07.920
But the doctor
knows that the winds

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:10.800
were only five miles
per hour that night,

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:13.200
which aren't strong enough
to move a grown woman.

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:18.560
Meanwhile, another
witness claims

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:21.560
to have allegedly seen
Ashley being followed

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:24.760
by a sunburned man the
night she went missing.

00:25:25.840 --> 00:25:28.880
(suspenseful music)

00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:34.920
Then, after nearly 10
hours of searching,

00:25:35.680 --> 00:25:38.360
the Coast Guard
spots Ashley's body.

00:25:44.600 --> 00:25:47.840
- So even though the
ocean is huge and vast,

00:25:47.840 --> 00:25:51.440
luckily Ashley's clothes
kept enough air inside

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:53.360
that her body stayed buoyant,

00:25:53.360 --> 00:25:55.440
and the Coast Guard was
able to discover her.

00:25:57.080 --> 00:25:59.360
- [Narrator] For police
this is a sign that Ashley

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:02.360
was either unconscious
or already dead

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:04.000
when she landed in the water.

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:09.720
As the medical examiner
inspects Ashley's body,

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:12.400
they find multiple examples
of physical injuries

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:16.280
sustained before she
was dumped overboard.

00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:18.360
- There is something
about knowing

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:20.760
that your daughter
is never coming back,

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:25.200
and there is knowing
that she's been found.

00:26:26.920 --> 00:26:29.400
- [Narrator] The medical
examiner finds a goose egg

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:33.320
type bump on Ashley's forehead
suggesting she was hit

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:36.080
with either a fist
or a blunt object.

00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:39.960
(dramatic music)

00:26:39.960 --> 00:26:42.960
They also find marks
on her neck along with

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:46.080
signs of hemorrhaging
in her neck and eyes,

00:26:46.080 --> 00:26:48.840
which all point to
manual strangulation.

00:26:51.760 --> 00:26:54.360
Ashley's clothing also
contained material

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:56.400
from the jogging
track on the deck,

00:26:56.400 --> 00:26:59.080
which means she was
being pressed down

00:26:59.080 --> 00:27:01.320
with considerable force,

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:03.800
potentially from someone
on top of her.

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:07.720
- What would bruising around
a person's neck indicate

00:27:07.720 --> 00:27:09.440
in a medical examiner's report?

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:11.400
It would tell me
it was a homicide.

00:27:12.560 --> 00:27:15.280
- [Narrator] With confirmation
from the medical examiner,

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:18.120
Dr. Price and staff
aboard the ship

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:22.240
can officially confirm they
have a murderer on board.

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:28.880
The questions now are who
killed Ashley and why?

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:31.720
(dramatic music)

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:41.160
- I can't even imagine her
not being in our life anymore.

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:46.720
- So because Charlie's been
kept in quarantine all this time

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:49.840
when the news breaks
that this investigation

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:52.000
has turned into a
murder investigation,

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:54.240
and there's something suspicious
with his wife's death,

00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:55.400
he's in the dark.

00:27:56.240 --> 00:27:57.400
He has no idea.

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:01.240
- [Narrator] With only
a couple hours remaining

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:04.440
before the cruise ship
reaches its final destination

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:06.320
for police to take over,

00:28:06.320 --> 00:28:10.280
the doctor decides it's time
to question his second suspect.

00:28:10.280 --> 00:28:13.000
Eric, the sunburned man,

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:16.680
who was spotted following
Ashley the night of her murder.

00:28:16.680 --> 00:28:19.360
(dramatic music)

00:28:20.680 --> 00:28:23.360
Eric is a Wisconsin
born accountant

00:28:23.360 --> 00:28:25.320
who tells the doctor
that he decided

00:28:25.320 --> 00:28:28.320
to take a cruise as a
way to meet single women,

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:33.320
but when the doctor confronts
him about being spotted

00:28:33.320 --> 00:28:36.960
following Ashley around
the ship, Eric denies it.

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:42.360
When the doctor tells Eric
that Ashley has been murdered

00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:44.960
and that he's a
potential suspect,

00:28:44.960 --> 00:28:47.160
Eric immediately comes clean.

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:51.160
- Eric, once he hears that
he's a suspect, realizes that

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:54.440
being embarrassed is way
better than doing prison time.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:58.040
So he comes forward
to the investigators,

00:28:58.040 --> 00:29:01.440
and admits that sure,
he had a crush on Ashley,

00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:03.880
and maybe he followed
her around too much.

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:07.080
He feels really badly about
that, but he has an alibi.

00:29:07.080 --> 00:29:08.920
He had nothing to
do with her murder.

00:29:10.400 --> 00:29:12.920
- [Narrator] Unfortunately
for Eric, his attempt

00:29:12.920 --> 00:29:16.240
to romance the tearful
newlywed doesn't go as planned.

00:29:17.920 --> 00:29:20.440
- It was embarrassing for
Eric to admit that Ashley

00:29:20.440 --> 00:29:24.960
even got angry with how
he was hovering over her

00:29:24.960 --> 00:29:26.200
and following her around.

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:32.760
Once she told him to leave
her alone, he did back off.

00:29:33.400 --> 00:29:35.400
(dramatic music)

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:40.840
He went to the bar that
night, got stinking drunk,

00:29:40.840 --> 00:29:42.440
and then was in his
berth throwing up

00:29:42.440 --> 00:29:43.920
for the rest of his night.

00:29:45.120 --> 00:29:46.760
(Eric coughing)
The brother heard

00:29:46.760 --> 00:29:48.120
and had to take care of him.

00:29:48.120 --> 00:29:51.440
So if his brother can
also verify his alibi.

00:29:51.440 --> 00:29:52.920
(dramatic music)

00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:54.280
- [Narrator] While
Dr. Price is happy

00:29:54.280 --> 00:29:56.440
to cross Eric off the list,

00:29:56.440 --> 00:29:59.280
he warns him against
approaching any women

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:01.440
for the remainder of the cruise

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:05.080
telling him to stay in his
room and out of the sun.

00:30:05.080 --> 00:30:07.800
(dramatic music)

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:11.280
Which leaves only one
remaining suspect.

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:17.320
- When Ashley's body is examined
by the medical examiner,

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:21.080
they discover that she still
has her engagement ring on,

00:30:22.480 --> 00:30:26.960
and its dimensions match the
scratches on Charlie's face.

00:30:29.240 --> 00:30:31.320
So that's pretty
damning evidence.

00:30:38.320 --> 00:30:41.160
(suspenseful music)

00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:28.200
- [Narrator] For the
doctor this final clue

00:31:28.200 --> 00:31:30.320
is the last piece
of evidence he needs

00:31:30.320 --> 00:31:32.080
to confirm his suspicions.

00:31:33.520 --> 00:31:36.360
On February 15, 1988,

00:31:36.360 --> 00:31:39.440
the ship reaches the port
of Long Beach, California.

00:31:42.040 --> 00:31:44.360
Police are waiting
to arrest Charlie

00:31:44.360 --> 00:31:46.480
for the murder of
his wife, Ashley.

00:31:52.720 --> 00:31:55.200
He's held without
bail until trial.

00:31:56.400 --> 00:31:58.480
- With such a strong
case against him,

00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:01.920
Charlie seems destined
for life in prison,

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:03.960
but after only
three days in jail,

00:32:05.080 --> 00:32:07.360
he comes to a
sudden realization.

00:32:07.360 --> 00:32:10.240
- [Narrator] Charlie demands
to speak with his lawyer.

00:32:12.320 --> 00:32:15.840
Charlie says he was framed
for his wife's death,

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:18.240
and what's more, he
knows who did it.

00:32:19.240 --> 00:32:21.280
(people chatting)

00:32:23.280 --> 00:32:26.640
On the outside Ashley
and Charlie appear to be

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:29.000
the perfect all-American couple,

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:31.960
combining their seven-day
honeymoon cruise

00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:34.280
with eye-catching
exercise routines.

00:32:35.800 --> 00:32:38.880
Until tragically the
honeymoon is cut short

00:32:38.880 --> 00:32:41.400
when Ashley's body
is found in the ocean

00:32:41.400 --> 00:32:43.960
after being strangled
and dumped overboard.

00:32:46.480 --> 00:32:48.800
Charlie denies his involvement,

00:32:48.800 --> 00:32:51.000
but with strong
evidence against him

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:53.760
and without a credible alibi,

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:55.880
he's arrested for
Ashley's murder.

00:32:57.680 --> 00:33:01.400
But after three days in jail,
Charlie calls his lawyer

00:33:01.400 --> 00:33:03.680
with some startling
new information.

00:33:05.240 --> 00:33:09.240
He says not only has he been
framed for his wife's murder,

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:12.680
he's figured out
who did it and why.

00:33:12.680 --> 00:33:15.400
(dramatic music)

00:33:17.840 --> 00:33:21.440
- There were these two
suspicious looking Ukrainian men

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:23.800
that were on the ship at
the same time as Charlie,

00:33:23.800 --> 00:33:25.840
and he's convinced
that they're the ones

00:33:25.840 --> 00:33:27.880
responsible for
his wife's murder.

00:33:29.840 --> 00:33:32.440
- [Narrator] Charlie tells
his lawyer that he believes

00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:34.960
Ashley's murder traces
back to a government

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:38.880
conspiracy that began
almost 11 years earlier.

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:44.720
- In 1978, Charlie
traveled to Ukraine

00:33:44.720 --> 00:33:47.880
with his parents to open
a chiropractic clinic.

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:50.400
This should be a new
beginning for the family.

00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:52.240
It should be a wonderful time,

00:33:52.240 --> 00:33:54.720
but somehow Charlie
ended up in jail

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:56.880
and a mental hospital
for two months.

00:33:59.560 --> 00:34:00.760
While he's there,

00:34:00.760 --> 00:34:02.840
he's allegedly
drugged and beaten

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:06.520
because he refuses to
marry a neighbor's niece.

00:34:06.520 --> 00:34:08.880
(dramatic music)

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:11.880
After he finally manages
to get back to the U.S.,

00:34:11.880 --> 00:34:13.800
he writes a book
about his experiences

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:16.360
and all the horror that
happened to him there.

00:34:19.360 --> 00:34:22.680
- So one of the things that
he said during the case

00:34:22.680 --> 00:34:25.400
was that the Ukraine
government was after him.

00:34:26.280 --> 00:34:28.280
(dramatic music)

00:34:28.280 --> 00:34:30.760
- So around the time
the book is published,

00:34:30.760 --> 00:34:34.040
the police contact Charlie's
parents with reports

00:34:34.040 --> 00:34:37.560
that someone has a
plot to kidnap Charlie

00:34:37.560 --> 00:34:38.920
outside a shopping mall.

00:34:40.760 --> 00:34:43.640
Charlie claimed that
two men grabbed him

00:34:43.640 --> 00:34:46.920
and said, "Ukraine is
here to take you away."

00:34:48.440 --> 00:34:50.840
But somehow Charlie manages
to break free and escape,

00:34:50.840 --> 00:34:53.800
even shooting one of
the would-be kidnappers.

00:34:54.920 --> 00:34:56.840
As he makes his bid for freedom,

00:34:57.960 --> 00:35:01.960
he then runs to his
car and drives away.

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:04.800
Charlie's eventually quoted
in a newspaper saying,

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:07.960
"All of Ukraine took their
best shot and they blew it."

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:12.880
This is why he believes
that the two men

00:35:12.880 --> 00:35:15.880
on the ship were actually
Ukrainian government agents

00:35:15.880 --> 00:35:18.640
who are there to
retaliate against him

00:35:18.640 --> 00:35:20.400
for writing this book,

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:22.640
and that's why they
murdered his wife.

00:35:23.760 --> 00:35:25.000
- [Narrator]
According to Charlie,

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:28.640
him and Ashley are exercising
on the running track

00:35:28.640 --> 00:35:30.960
when his wife is
viciously attacked

00:35:30.960 --> 00:35:33.360
by a stranger and
his accomplice.

00:35:34.920 --> 00:35:37.960
(suspenseful music)

00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:54.920
- As a writer, a
writer of fiction,

00:35:54.920 --> 00:35:56.920
I have a hard time
with this story.

00:35:56.920 --> 00:35:58.840
It seems a little
too good to be true,

00:35:59.800 --> 00:36:01.800
and this is such a great story,

00:36:01.800 --> 00:36:05.440
these Ukrainian government
agents, perfect story.

00:36:05.440 --> 00:36:08.600
So why did Charlie
first have that yarn

00:36:08.600 --> 00:36:10.600
about his wife being
blown over the rail?

00:36:10.600 --> 00:36:12.680
Why not tell the truth
from the beginning?

00:36:14.320 --> 00:36:16.800
But Charlie of
course has a theory.

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:19.520
Of course, he must
have been drugged.

00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:23.880
That's why he thought he saw
his wife blown over the edge.

00:36:23.880 --> 00:36:25.960
It was because he was drugged.

00:36:25.960 --> 00:36:28.680
(dramatic music)

00:36:31.960 --> 00:36:34.840
- [Narrator] It's an incredible
story, and if it's true,

00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:37.480
it'll all come out
during Charlie's trial.

00:36:38.560 --> 00:36:41.440
(dramatic music)

00:36:41.440 --> 00:36:44.320
The prosecution outlines
a straightforward case

00:36:44.320 --> 00:36:48.200
against Charlie, first by
using the forensic evidence

00:36:48.200 --> 00:36:50.560
provided by the
medical examiner,

00:36:50.560 --> 00:36:53.440
as well as testimony
from the cruise ship crew

00:36:53.440 --> 00:36:57.400
and passengers who witnessed
the couple arguing.

00:36:57.400 --> 00:37:00.240
(people chatting)

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:04.400
But Charlie maintains
his innocence,

00:37:04.400 --> 00:37:08.880
resting his entire defense on
the idea of him being framed

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:10.680
by the Ukrainian passengers.

00:37:13.360 --> 00:37:16.600
But the prosecution
surprises everyone

00:37:16.600 --> 00:37:19.760
when they bring one of the
Ukrainian men to the stand.

00:37:21.280 --> 00:37:23.760
- It turns out there's
only two Ukrainian people

00:37:23.760 --> 00:37:25.760
on this entire cruise ship,

00:37:25.760 --> 00:37:29.600
and one of them decides to
testify to clear his name.

00:37:30.840 --> 00:37:32.560
He's not a secret agent.

00:37:32.560 --> 00:37:34.600
He's got no connection
to the government.

00:37:34.600 --> 00:37:36.200
He's just a wedding photographer

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:38.400
who is there for a
vacation with his friend.

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:42.920
The story of the photographers
is definitely true.

00:37:42.920 --> 00:37:44.640
They were photographers
just there

00:37:44.640 --> 00:37:46.600
to take a picture of a wedding,

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:48.920
and it was a friend's wedding.

00:37:50.720 --> 00:37:53.280
- [Narrator] The prosecution
closes their case

00:37:53.280 --> 00:37:56.720
by outlining exactly
how they believe Charlie

00:37:56.720 --> 00:37:58.760
killed his wife Ashley.

00:37:58.760 --> 00:38:01.520
(dramatic music)

00:38:03.400 --> 00:38:06.320
It all starts with a bad
argument over dinner,

00:38:07.800 --> 00:38:09.880
the couple tries to
put it behind them,

00:38:11.880 --> 00:38:13.920
but during a late-night jog,

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:16.760
Charlie's rage boils over.

00:38:16.760 --> 00:38:19.440
(ominous music)

00:38:24.800 --> 00:38:27.760
First, Charlie hits
Ashley in the head,

00:38:27.760 --> 00:38:29.480
knocking her earring off,

00:38:29.480 --> 00:38:31.320
sending her crashing
to the deck.

00:38:33.640 --> 00:38:36.840
Then he climbs on top
of her and strangles her

00:38:36.840 --> 00:38:39.320
pushing her down
into the ship's deck

00:38:39.320 --> 00:38:41.720
and holding her until
she's unconscious.

00:38:44.840 --> 00:38:48.680
Then Charlie carries her
body to the rail of the ship

00:38:48.680 --> 00:38:51.480
and tosses her overboard
(water sloshing)

00:38:51.480 --> 00:38:53.400
while she's still unconscious.

00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:58.720
Her official cause of
death is listed as drowning

00:38:58.720 --> 00:39:00.960
by way of manual strangulation.

00:39:02.480 --> 00:39:05.240
(dramatic music)

00:39:10.880 --> 00:39:12.880
- Even with all the
evidence against him,

00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:18.920
Charlie sticks with
his Ukrainian story.

00:39:18.920 --> 00:39:20.640
He tells the jury
that even though

00:39:20.640 --> 00:39:24.480
his story may seem contrived,
these things do happen

00:39:24.480 --> 00:39:26.520
in the world of
international intrigue.

00:39:29.120 --> 00:39:31.600
- [Narrator] Is it possible
that Ashley's murder

00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:35.280
is part of a targeted Ukrainian
government conspiracy,

00:39:36.680 --> 00:39:39.040
or was Ashley's
death nothing more

00:39:39.040 --> 00:39:41.360
than a tragic
cold-blooded murder?

00:39:44.560 --> 00:39:46.600
It will be up to
the jury to decide.

00:39:47.520 --> 00:39:49.760
(dramatic music)

00:39:54.160 --> 00:39:57.880
Charlie is on trial for the
murder of his wife Ashley.

00:39:57.880 --> 00:40:00.720
He's accused of murdering
her on their honeymoon.

00:40:02.480 --> 00:40:05.000
The prosecution has a solid case

00:40:05.000 --> 00:40:08.360
backed up by medical evidence
and witness testimony,

00:40:09.800 --> 00:40:12.240
but Charlie says he's been
framed for the murder.

00:40:13.320 --> 00:40:15.400
He claims that his wife's death

00:40:15.400 --> 00:40:19.400
is in fact a retaliation
by the Ukrainian government

00:40:19.400 --> 00:40:22.320
for a book he wrote about
them a year earlier.

00:40:28.480 --> 00:40:30.280
With both arguments presented,

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:33.080
all that's left is for
the jury to determine

00:40:33.080 --> 00:40:35.400
if they believe in
Charlie's Ukrainian

00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:37.880
government retaliation
killing theory,

00:40:38.600 --> 00:40:41.440
or that he's the murderer,

00:40:41.440 --> 00:40:44.800
willing to say whatever he
can to stay out of jail.

00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:47.760
(dramatic music)

00:40:54.480 --> 00:40:56.480
- So Charlie's
got a big problem.

00:40:57.360 --> 00:40:59.360
His story is full of holes,

00:40:59.360 --> 00:41:01.440
and everything'S
just not adding up.

00:41:03.120 --> 00:41:04.600
There's so much against him.

00:41:04.600 --> 00:41:08.120
People saw him arguing with
his wife the night she died.

00:41:08.120 --> 00:41:11.400
The forensic evidence,
scratches on his face,

00:41:11.400 --> 00:41:14.160
the fact that there's signs
that she was strangled,

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:17.600
the alibi that the
Ukrainians had,

00:41:17.600 --> 00:41:20.960
so they couldn't possibly
be the ones that drugged him

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:22.680
and threw his wife overboard.

00:41:24.240 --> 00:41:26.120
All of this is
pointing to Charlie.

00:41:27.040 --> 00:41:29.440
- [Narrator] Charlie Gerber's
espionage defense

00:41:29.440 --> 00:41:32.120
is not enough to
sway the jury,

00:41:32.120 --> 00:41:34.880
who find him guilty of
second degree murder

00:41:34.880 --> 00:41:36.960
on the high seas.

00:41:36.960 --> 00:41:38.600
(people chatting)

00:41:38.600 --> 00:41:41.480
- In the end the judge said
he'd never seen a honeymoon

00:41:41.480 --> 00:41:44.320
that ended in such a chilling
and heartless manner.

00:41:45.960 --> 00:41:47.160
He called Ashley's murder

00:41:47.160 --> 00:41:49.400
one of the cruelest
crimes he'd ever seen.

00:41:52.920 --> 00:41:54.680
- Your daughter
can never come back,

00:41:54.680 --> 00:41:57.160
but to do have closure
in knowing that

00:41:57.160 --> 00:41:59.120
Charlie was arrested
and convicted.

00:42:00.600 --> 00:42:02.960
- [Narrator] Charlie is
sentenced to life in prison

00:42:02.960 --> 00:42:06.080
without parole for the
murder of his wife Ashley.

00:42:07.960 --> 00:42:11.320
It's a senseless and tragic
end to what should have been

00:42:11.320 --> 00:42:14.520
a beautiful start to a
beautiful life together.

00:42:14.520 --> 00:42:16.040
(people applauding)

00:42:16.040 --> 00:42:18.560
(dramatic music)

00:42:24.040 --> 00:42:26.520
(water sloshing)

00:42:31.120 --> 00:42:33.880
(dramatic music)

